    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Prose in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>Prose, derived from the Latin word <em>"prosa,"</em> meaning 'straightforward,' is the cornerstone of effective and accessible language.</p>
<p>Imagine having a conversation in a plain, direct manner—that's prose in action. This guide explores the world of prose, its definition, everyday applications, and versatility in both written and spoken communication.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>What is Prose?</strong></h1>
<p>Prose is the conversational heart of language. It's how we communicate directly and without the lyrical structures of poetry.</p>
<p>Unlike poetry, which adheres to intricate rhythms and patterns, prose has a loosely defined structure, making it the language of practicality and clarity.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Applications of Prose</strong></h1>
<ol>
<li><strong>Books</strong>: Novels, textbooks, and non-fiction use prose to convey stories and ideas clearly.</li>
<li><strong>Spoken Dialogue</strong>: Everyday conversations with friends, coworkers, or family rely on prose.</li>
<li><strong>Speeches</strong>: Motivational speeches, TED Talks, and political addresses use prose for clarity and persuasion.</li>
<li><strong>Factual Writing</strong>: News articles, reports, and essays present information in prose for straightforward communication.</li>
<li><strong>Fiction</strong>: Fictional narratives use prose for storytelling while incorporating poetic elements for artistic effect.</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Forms of Prose</strong></h1>
<ol>
<li><strong>Narrative Prose</strong>: Tells a story and focuses on characters and events.</li>
<li><strong>Descriptive Prose</strong>: Paints vivid images using words, often found in creative writing.</li>
<li><strong>Expository Prose</strong>: Explains or informs, as seen in essays, textbooks, and reports.</li>
<li><strong>Argumentative Prose</strong>: Persuades or argues a point, common in debates and editorials.</li>
<li><strong>Conversational Prose</strong>: Mimics everyday speech and is used in dialogue.</li>
<li><strong>Literary Prose</strong>: Focuses on the beauty of language, as seen in classic literature.</li>
<li><strong>Technical Prose</strong>: Precise and formal, found in manuals and research papers.</li>
<li><strong>Stream of Consciousness</strong>: Captures flowing thoughts and emotions, often in modernist literature.</li>
<li><strong>Journalistic Prose</strong>: Clear and direct, used in news reporting.</li>
<li><strong>Autobiographical Prose</strong>: Found in memoirs and autobiographies, sharing life experiences.</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Prose</strong></h1>
<h2><strong>1. Fiction</strong></h2>
<p>Fiction refers to imaginative storytelling that isn’t based on real events or facts. Types include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Novel</strong>: Extended works (50,000–150,000+ words) with complex plots and character development.</li>
<li><strong>Novella</strong>: Shorter than a novel (20,000–50,000 words), offering a concise yet rich narrative.</li>
<li><strong>Short Story</strong>: Brief works (a few hundred to 20,000 words) focusing on a single idea or moment.</li>
<li><strong>Flash Fiction</strong>: Extremely short stories that present narratives in a limited space.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Characteristics of Fiction</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Imaginative creation.</li>
<li>Narrative structure.</li>
<li>Character development.</li>
<li>Exploration of varied themes.</li>
<li>Escapist qualities.</li>
</ul>
<h2><strong>2. Non-Fiction</strong></h2>
<p>Non-fiction is grounded in reality, documenting actual events and experiences. Types include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Essays</strong>: Blend personal viewpoints with factual information.</li>
<li><strong>Biographies and Autobiographies</strong>: Life stories of individuals, either by others or self-written.</li>
<li><strong>Memoirs</strong>: Focus on specific life experiences.</li>
<li><strong>Journalism</strong>: Reporting of news and current events.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Characteristics of Non-Fiction</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Factual basis.</li>
<li>Educational and insightful.</li>
<li>Personal or research-based.</li>
<li>Objective and subjective elements.</li>
</ul>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>General Characteristics of Prose</strong></h1>
<p><strong>1. Subject Matter</strong></p>
<p>The subject matter is the main idea of the story. It’s what the story is about. It differs from the theme, which deals with deeper, abstract ideas that are explored through the subject matter.</p>
<p><strong>2. Characters and Characterization</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Character</strong>: A character can be a person, animal, or object in the story. They drive the plot and are central to the narrative.</li>
<li><strong>Characterization</strong>: This is how characters are developed through their actions, thoughts, and dialogue.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Types of Characters:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confidante</strong>: Someone the main character trusts and confides in.</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic Character</strong>: A character who changes over time.</li>
<li><strong>Static Character</strong>: A character who stays the same.</li>
<li><strong>Antagonist</strong>: The character or force that opposes the protagonist.</li>
<li><strong>Protagonist</strong>: The main character who drives the story.</li>
<li><strong>Round Character</strong>: A well-developed character with depth.</li>
<li><strong>Flat Character</strong>: A simple, one-dimensional character.</li>
<li><strong>Stock Character</strong>: A stereotypical, unchanging character.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Conflict</strong></p>
<p>Conflict is the struggle between opposing forces that drives the plot. It can be internal (within a character) or external (between characters or forces).</p>
<p><strong>4. Plot</strong></p>
<p>The plot is the sequence of events in a story. A typical plot follows this structure:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Exposition</strong>: Introduction of characters and setting.</li>
<li><strong>Rising Action</strong>: The conflict develops.</li>
<li><strong>Climax</strong>: The peak of the conflict.</li>
<li><strong>Falling Action</strong>: Events leading to resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Denouement</strong>: The conflict is resolved.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Types of Plot</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Simple Plot</strong>: A straightforward story.</li>
<li><strong>Complex Plot</strong>: Multiple storylines and obstacles.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>5. Theme</strong></p>
<p>The theme is the underlying message or main idea of the story. It reflects the author’s perspective on life and is often linked to the central conflict.</p>
<p><strong>6. Setting</strong></p>
<p>The setting is the time and place where the story takes place. It affects the mood and influences the characters and events. It can be fictional or based on real locations.</p>
<p><strong>7. Narrative Techniques / Point of View</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>First-Person</strong>: The story is told from the perspective of a character using "I."</li>
<li><strong>Third-Person Omniscient</strong>: The narrator knows all characters' thoughts and feelings.</li>
<li><strong>Mixed</strong>: Multiple narrative techniques used in the same story.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple Narrators</strong>: The story is told from various characters’ viewpoints.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>8. Tone</strong></p>
<p>Tone is the attitude of the writer toward the subject. It can be serious, humorous, sarcastic, etc. It shapes how readers feel while reading.</p>
<p><strong>9. Mood</strong></p>
<p>Mood is the emotional atmosphere of the story. It is created by the setting, tone, and diction and makes the reader feel a certain way, such as happy, sad, or suspenseful.</p>`
